  "conclusions": [
    "The unchanged synthetic thresholds reject all192 TEST scenes for allfour methods. Zero false positives here coincides with zero accepted positives; it is not useful detection.",
    "The native learned-proposal hybrid has zero threshold-free correct selected boxes among96 positive TEST photos.82 have no proposal at IoU>=.5; the other14 contain a suitable proposal but a different box ranks first. Thus changing only the acceptance threshold cannot produce localized true positives with these selected boxes.",
    "The fixed51window diagnostic raises suitable-proposal coverage from14/96 to64/96, yet native matching still selects no correct box. Both proposal coverage and ranking transfer are observed limitations.",
    "The old CNN selects1/96 correct box with learned proposals and6/96 with fixed grids before cutoff. Both native and CNN pooled presence AUROC values are close to.5 on this cohort; no broad category-recognition claim follows.",
    "RealDEV threshold calibration accepts7/96 positive TEST scenes and2/96 negative scenes for native matching, but its7 accepted positive boxes are still incorrectly localized. Calibration alone does not resolve localization.",
    "All12 references produce non-null foreground descriptions and4\u201312 selected patches. Only50/3717 learned TEST proposals have unresolved regions. This is not primarily a null-input/numerical-path failure; a resolved region can still represent the wrong semantic content.",
    "Independent arithmetic audit finds exact agreement for original-coordinate grids, reference transformations and all selected/best-proposal IoUs. Learned proposal inverse-coordinate residual is at most5.7e-14. These checks do not support a coordinate-bookkeeping explanation.",
    "Real-photo clutter, object/background segmentation, different physical exemplars, viewpoint, fine appearance and semantic category diversity changed together. This test cannot isolate compression as the sole cause or prove one specific mechanism.",
    "The frozen matcher was designed to retain and align tiny details on synthetic shape identity tasks. Natural category recognition requires accepting legitimate appearance changes across distinct examples; this test demonstrates that the current model has not achieved that objective."
  ],
